From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oliver Sargison <sargo@sargo.cc>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 11:23:52 +1300
Subject: [PATCH 15/15] Plutonium: extensions stay out of the shell

Extensions load in shell mode, and the shell page was an ordinary page to them: a tab in an app window, listed by chrome.tabs, given every <all_urls> content script, open to scripting.executeScript, and its requests visible to webRequest and declarativeNetRequest. The window broker answers in every world of a shell document, so any extension allowed on all sites could drive every window and tab, or redirect the shell's own scripts. Now:
- the renderer injects no extension script into a shell document or a frame of it (ExtensionInjectionHost; asked of the document, so shells added later are covered);
- in the browser, the shells' origins are restricted URLs to every extension (a PermissionsData policy delegate), so no script access, debugger or capture;
- a shell page's requests, and requests for one, are hidden from webRequest and declarativeNetRequest, as privileged content's are;
- a shell's window isn't in the extensions' window list, its page isn't found by tab id (not even through a prerender in it), and it raises no tab events.

It holds these changes:
- extensions can't script the shell page
